The attached image illustrates the transient inflammatory response induced by Nano-11 at the injection site and its uptake by various recruited immune cells, providing context for Nano-11's role as a vaccine adjuvant. Panel A, a line graph, quantifies different inflammatory cell types (neutrophils, Mac-2+ macrophages, antigen-presenting cells, eosinophils, Ly6C+ monocytes) at the injection site over 21 days post-injection, showing an initial rise and subsequent decline in cell numbers, indicating a transient inflammation. Panel B presents confocal microscopy images that demonstrate Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 (red) being phagocytized and present within MHCII+, Ly6C+, and Mac-2+ cells at various time points, confirming the efficient uptake of Nano-11 by these immune cells at the site of injection.